  "prompt_text": "The figure outlines a host response pathway linking infection, inflammation, adipose tissue, and immune-cell growth. A pathogen infects a host cell, which then secretes TNF. This TNF signal acts on adipose tissue through TNF receptors, triggering two parallel outputs: secretion of leptin and lipolysis.\n\nLeptin is shown acting on leukocytes via the leptin receptor, suggesting a direct signaling role in these immune cells. At the same time, lipolysis releases fatty acids and glycerol, which are indicated as carbon substrates that also support leukocytes. Together, these two adipose-derived responses appear to promote leukocyte proliferation.\n\nOverall, the image presents adipose tissue as an active mediator of immune defense rather than just an energy store. Inflammatory TNF produced after infection stimulates adipose tissue, which in turn supplies both a hormone-like signal, leptin, and metabolic fuel from fat breakdown. These inputs converge on leukocytes and are associated with increased proliferation. The main visible message is that infection-induced TNF can indirectly boost immune-cell expansion by reprogramming adipose tissue to provide both signaling and nutrients.",
